      "message": "Why do you need to do this extra tracking?\n\nI think there are only two cases.\n\n1. An NRI is live and is referred to by one or more entries in mNetworkRequests, mDefaultNetworkRequests, has some requests in it, etc. When this method runs the first time, it will remove all such entries and unlink the death recipient.\n2. The NRI is no longer live and not referred to by any entries in mNetworkRequests. If this method is called, then it will not find any such entries, and there will be nothing to do.\n\nThis method cannot run concurrently with itself, so there should really only be two cases. The first time it runs, it\u0027s case #1. The second time, it\u0027s case #2.\n\nThis tracked boolean guarantees that the code will skip cleanup the second time around. However, the fact that the code crashes if cleanup is attempted twice is a red flag that suggests that said cleanup code is incorrect.\n\nInstead of adding the tracked boolean, I think it\u0027s much better to make this method correctly clear all state, such that if this method is called again, it\u0027s a no-op. That would give us more confidence that all our bookkeeping / reference counting / ... is correct, because we could put assertions / wtfs in our code that would complain loudly, for example, if an NRI with live requests is freed.\n\nFor example: you could make decrementRequestCount return the decremented number and complain loudly if it\u0027s greater than zero.",
